    "Analytical Sexualist":
      '<p>For the Analytical Sexualist, libido challenges are <strong>rarely about sex itself</strong>.<br />They are about <strong>clarity, predictability, and whether the erotic "system" makes sense</strong>.</p><p>Their desire is highly coherence-dependent. When signals are clear and communication is direct, libido can be strong, intentional, and consistent. When ambiguity or emotional chaos increases, desire often stalls into analysis, long before they can access the body\'s yes.</p><p>According to the archetype model, the Analytical Sexualist\'s libido is especially sensitive to:</p><p><strong>Ambiguity and unclear signals<br /></strong>When partners expect mind-reading, libido drops. Uncertainty activates thinking, not arousal.</p><p><strong>Over-analysis and evaluation loops<br /></strong>If they\'re busy measuring or troubleshooting, they can\'t feel spontaneous desire. The mind crowds out the body.</p><p><strong>Relational chaos or unresolved puzzles<br /></strong>Stress, confusion, or unresolved conflict keeps libido offline. They need the system to feel solvable and stable.</p><p><strong>Shame about their thinking style<br /></strong>If a partner criticizes their planning or "being in their head," libido can shut down in defensiveness or embarrassment.</p><p>Libido challenges for the Analytical Sexualist often show up as: difficulty accessing spontaneous arousal, desire staying active in solo contexts but dropping in messy relational space, avoidance when communication is vague, frustration replacing erotic flow, confusion or self-doubt ("Why can\'t I just relax into it?").</p><p>Importantly, attraction to the partner may still be present. The shutdown is <strong>situational</strong>, not relational rejection.</p><p>These libido challenges are frequently misread as: low libido, sexual inhibition, lack of passion, loss of attraction.</p><p>Partners may interpret the shutdown as personal rejection. Analytical Sexualists themselves often internalize blame, assuming they are "too cerebral," "too picky," or "unable to feel."</p><p>In reality, their libido is <strong>protective and context-sensitive</strong>. It withdraws when the system becomes unclear or shame-based.</p><p>As outlined in the attached material, the Analytical Sexualist has a <strong>moderate risk orientation</strong>. Their desire thrives in clarity, clean feedback, and stable communication, not in ambiguity, chaos, or shame about their process</p><p>When relationships drift toward: low communication and mind-reading expectations, emotional volatility and unresolved puzzles, criticism of their thinking style, chaotic initiation without clarity.</p><p>libido often collapses, not as a conscious choice, but as nervous-system regulation.</p><p>For the Analytical Sexualist, healing libido challenges does not involve: trying harder, increasing frequency, forcing spontaneity.</p><p>Healthy restoration comes from: making desire discussable without killing the mood, clarifying signals, consent, and preferences, reducing shame around planning and feedback, and creating stability so the body can follow.</p><p>As clarity, safety, and embodied trust deepen, libido often <strong>returns organically</strong>. Their desire does not need pressure; it needs coherence.</p><p>Over time, this can expand into: more ease shifting from mind to body, clearer communication about what helps arousal, and less self-blame when desire fluctuates.</p><p>The goal is not higher libido, but <strong>libido that feels clear rather than confusing</strong>.</p><p> For the Analytical Sexualist, libido challenges are not signs of dysfunction. They are <strong>signals that the conditions for desire are missing</strong>.</p><p>Desire re-emerges when: clarity is restored, pressure is removed, and sex becomes a collaborative system rather than an unsolved puzzle.</p>',
    "Quiet Withdrawer":
      '<p>For the Quiet Withdrawer, libido challenges are <strong>rarely about sex itself</strong>.<br />They are about <strong>safety, autonomy, and whether the nervous system can relax at all</strong>.</p><p>Their desire is highly regulation-dependent. When life is calm and connection feels safe, libido can appear slowly and steadily. When conflict, pressure, or overwhelm is present, desire often goes offline almost completely, long before they can access words for what\'s happening.</p><p>According to the archetype model, the Quiet Withdrawer\'s libido is especially sensitive to:</p><p><strong>Baseline anxiety and overload<br /></strong>Chronic stress can keep libido under-activated. When the system is already taxed, arousal becomes inaccessible.</p><p><strong>Conflict and emotional tension<br /></strong>Frequent conflict can erase desire. Even low-level relational tension can keep the body guarded.</p><p><strong>Pressure and fear of being pressed into sex<br /></strong>If affection is interpreted as a "lead-up" that they can\'t control, they may avoid closeness altogether, further reducing libido.</p><p><strong>Mismatch in arousal pacing<br /></strong>Their slow curve can collide with partner expectations. When they feel rushed, libido shuts down as self-protection.</p><p>Libido challenges for the Quiet Withdrawer often show up as: near-total disappearance of desire during conflict, avoidance of affectionate situations that might lead to pressure, internal desire that doesn\'t translate into initiation, numbness or shutdown when pushed, confusion or self-doubt ("Why is my body not responding?").</p><p>Importantly, attraction to the partner may still be present. The shutdown is <strong>situational</strong>, not relational rejection.</p><p>These libido challenges are frequently misread as: low libido, sexual inhibition, lack of passion, loss of attraction.</p><p>Partners may interpret the shutdown as personal rejection. Quiet Withdrawers themselves often internalize blame, assuming they are "broken," "cold," or "not made for sex."</p><p>In reality, their libido is <strong>protective and context-sensitive</strong>. It withdraws when safety and autonomy feel threatened.</p><p>As outlined in the attached material, the Quiet Withdrawer has a <strong>low risk orientation</strong>. Their desire thrives in calm, safety, and non-pressure autonomy, not in conflict, urgency, or expectation</p><p>When relationships drift toward: frequent conflict and tension, pressure around sex, overstimulation and rushed pacing, affection that feels like a trap.</p><p>libido often collapses, not as a conscious choice, but as nervous-system regulation.</p><p>For the Quiet Withdrawer, healing libido challenges does not involve: trying harder, increasing frequency, forcing initiation.</p><p>Healthy restoration comes from: reducing conflict and repairing safety, removing pressure and entitlement cues, slowing pacing and honoring autonomy, and rebuilding non-sexual closeness without expectation.</p><p>As calm, trust, and nervous-system regulation deepen, libido often <strong>returns organically</strong>. Their desire does not need stimulation; it needs safety to emerge.</p><p>Over time, this can expand into: more confidence naming needs and boundaries, clearer pacing agreements with partners, and less self-blame when desire fluctuates.</p><p>The goal is not higher libido, but <strong>libido that feels safe rather than coerced</strong>.</p><p> For the Quiet Withdrawer, libido challenges are not signs of dysfunction. They are <strong>signals that the conditions for desire are missing</strong>.</p><p>Desire re-emerges when: safety is restored, pressure is removed, and sex becomes an invitation rather than an expectation.</p><p>Understanding this pattern transforms libido challenges from a problem to be fixed into <strong>information about how desire works in this system</strong>.</p>',
